 "tldr": "Block f01 of the q4 common-state reading was quarantined by ONE state, 7411758182009c20 (airline task 30, t = 6, source a6k2_q4_bfpair_f01), failing the implicit identity in both tasks. a_d2 is a different token sequence from a_BF16 and a_d1: the same 96 tokens except that the keys of the nested flights[0] object come in a different order (d2: flight_number, origin, destination, date; BF16 and d1: flight_number, date, origin, destination). canon_msg compares parsed arguments as Python dicts, which ignores key order, so a_d2 counted as canonically equal and the implicit checks applied. The chat template renders arguments in insertion order, so the t = 7 prompt differs in 24 of 7,016 token positions: 600f0c14 vs 211d510b. Re-tokenizing with the swapped order reproduces both recorded hashes exactly, and the continuations are therefore not replays. Every serving-side check at this state passed, so this is not numerical non-determinism. It is a comparison artifact: the precondition of the check is canonical equality, which is weaker than token or render identity. Under the frozen rule the quarantine was correctly triggered. Identity checks over all q4+q8 common-state tasks (gate + panel): implicit 869/871, reference re-runs 369/369, together 1,238/1,240 (99.84 %, Wilson 95 % [99.41, 99.96]). d2 replay 1,578/1,578, cross-task a_d2 789/789. Both failures are this one state.",
